From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felipe Paiva <felipe.paiva@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:39:59 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] ps1-discs

PlayStation discs swap through the libretro disk-control interface. slot
answers the legacy interface (13), the interface version (57, version 1)
and the EXT interface (58), and keeps the core's callbacks in HostExtra; they are used only where
the platform swaps discs (PlayStation), so a Sega or NES core that
registers the interface is never asked about discs.
A swap opens the tray, sets the index and closes the tray; an index out of
range or one the core refuses goes back to the disc that was in, with a
"Disc swap failed" toast. SELECT+L2 swaps to the next disc on PlayStation
only, because L2 and R2 belong to the game and the in-game menus are pinned
by upstream tests; everywhere else SELECT+L2 still steps the palette back.

The disc in play is written to Saves/PS1/<stem>.disc on every swap and
handed to set_initial_image before the next load, so a game restarts on
the disc it was left on; a missing, unreadable or out-of-range file means
disc 1, logged. A state of a game with more than one disc ends with
SLOTPSX1 and the disc index as a little-endian u32. Loading it strips the
trailer and swaps to that disc first when it is not the disc in play; a
state with no trailer loads as the core reads it, and a trailer of an
unknown version is refused with a toast. Single-disc states are exactly
the core's bytes.

The swap with rollback follows bently0602/slot, and the initial disc and
the remembered disc file follow AaronC1990/tray and oauramos/omega-zero
(all GPL-3.0).
